Title: THE FLASH
Credit: Filmstrip screenplay prepared from supplied transcript
Source: Pasted text.txt — Chapter 4: 2302: The Flash
Draft date: November 17, 2026
Premise: Three generation ships enter interstellar space together. When a tiny impact destroys one, the survivors separate to prevent another shared catastrophe.
Setting: The year 2302, aboard and around three generation ships leaving the Solar System for Tau Ceti. The passage moves from Neptune into interstellar space, ending with the two surviving ships widely separated.

[[Production: Narration preserved word for word from the supplied chapter; timestamps removed. One offscreen narrator. Sixteen spoken pictures, one per spoken sentence, plus six silent pictures marked on purpose: they are the silences and set pieces of the finished video (about 20 seconds in all). No automatic holds.]]
[[Visual style: Warm miniature painted environments and spacecraft, tactile matte surfaces, fine paper grain, understated dimensional lighting; no photorealism, lettering or labels.]]
[[Continuity: Three identical generation ships, each with a forward shield disk, a habitation ring on a long spine and a stern engine, told apart only by hull stripes. The Hesperos has a RED stripe; the two ships that survive have ORANGE stripes. The narrator's cabin has a desk lamp, a monitor, a round porthole, an unmade bed, a small handheld recorder with a red light and an orange scarf over the chair back; the narrator is never shown.]]
[[Timing: Original transcript passage starts are not sentence-aligned audio timings. Picture duration follows the recorded delivery; only the silent pictures carry a Hold.]]

EXT. INTERSTELLAR SPACE - 2302

[[Sentence: flash-s001]]
[[Pic: Extreme wide: three long generation ships fly past the big blue disk of Neptune, engine plumes glowing; two have orange hull stripes and one has a red stripe. Neptune dominates the background.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In our second year, we passed Neptune, nearly three billion miles from home.

[[Sentence: flash-s002]]
[[Pic: Wide rear view: the three small ships sit inside a vast widening cone of pale-blue solar-wind streaks that fall away behind them, the Sun a tiny point at its tip. The haze is illustrative, not a solid wall.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Weeks later, we crossed the edge of the Sun's wind, where interstellar space begins.

[[Pic: Medium side view: one orange-striped ship alone in empty black space, engine glowing, the blue haze gone behind it.]]
[[Silent on purpose: the ships have crossed the edge; a short breath before the comparison.]]
[[Hold: 2s.]]
The lead ship glides on alone through clear black space.

[[Sentence: flash-s003]]
[[Pic: A rocket lifts off from a coastal launch site on a pillar of flame above a wide cloud of smoke, drawn like an old sepia photograph; the 1977 launch as a historical comparison, with no lettering or dates.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Voyager 1, a probe launched from Earth in 1977, had needed thirty-five years to get that far.

[[Sentence: flash-s004]]
[[Pic: Wide angled view: the three ships travel in formation, stern fusion plumes blazing blue-white, the two orange-striped ships and the red-striped one distinct; the distant stars stay points, with no speed streaks.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Soon we were moving at a tenth of the speed of light.

[[Sentence: flash-s005]]
[[Pic: Close: one ship's huge stern engine cone, dark and inert, ringed by pale fuel tanks, a faint blue glow deep inside fading out; the edge of the habitation ring curves at the right.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then the engines went quiet.

INT. SHUTTLE HANGAR - 2302

[[Sentence: flash-s006]]
[[Pic: Interior wide: a hangar bay with a wall open to black space; a small shuttle rests on the deck and a sister ship hangs close outside the opening, near enough to cross to.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The three ships glided on in silence, close together, so shuttles could carry spare parts and doctors across.

EXT. INTERSTELLAR SPACE - 2302

[[Sentence: flash-s007]]
[[Pic: Macro foreground: one small irregular grey pebble drifts alone in black space, with the whole Solar System as a faint line of tiny points far behind it; the size contrast makes the threat legible.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At this speed, a pebble of one gram would hit like over a hundred tons of TNT.

[[Pic: Close: a pebble strikes the forward shield disk of a ship in a blinding white flash, and a rolling orange fireball and sparks spread across the frame.]]
[[Silent on purpose: shows the TNT comparison as an imagined hit, so the claim lands before the next idea.]]
[[Hold: 2.5s.]]
An imagined impact blooms against the shield.

[[Sentence: flash-s008]]
[[Pic: High oblique view: the three ships as a staggered convoy against a band of the Milky Way, the red-striped ship forward and the two orange-striped ships behind, so the lead position is clear in one picture.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So the ships took turns in front, every few months.

[[Sentence: flash-s009]]
[[Pic: Closer three-quarter front view: the red-striped Hesperos fills the right of the frame, shield disk nearest the viewer and ring spokes visible; the two orange-striped ships follow small behind at upper left.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In those weeks, the Hesperos led.

INT. CABIN - NIGHT

[[Sentence: flash-s010]]
[[Pic: Interior wide: a small night cabin; a desk lamp glows over a monitor showing two distant points of light, a round porthole shows stars, the bed is unmade, a handheld recorder with a red light sits on the desk and an orange scarf hangs over the chair back; nobody is visible.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Just before I fell asleep, I was watching the sister ships on my screen...

[[Sentence: flash-s011]]
[[Pic: Close on the round porthole, dark and full of stars; one point of light low on the left begins to bloom white.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then the window flashed.

[[Pic: Interior: the whole cabin flooded with blinding white light from the porthole; the lamp, desk, recorder and scarf are only dark outlines against it.]]
[[Silent on purpose: the flash itself and the moment after it, before anyone can say what happened.]]
[[Hold: 4s.]]
White light fills the cabin.

EXT. INTERSTELLAR SPACE - 2302

[[Sentence: flash-s012]]
[[Pic: Exterior wide: a bright pink-white burst at the centre of a red-tinged sky, a large intact ring section drifting at upper right and long torn hull sections tumbling at left, stars and a faint galaxy band behind.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A stone the size of a pea had torn the Hesperos apart.

[[Pic: Wide: a vast torn slab of the Hesperos hull, its red stripe running along it and its broken edge glowing, drifts across the frame with white light spilling beyond.]]
[[Silent on purpose: the scale of the loss, held in silence.]]
[[Hold: 4s.]]
The wreck turns slowly in the light.

[[Pic: Wide, seen through the round rim of a window: red embers, a broken ring fragment and torn plates drift in a dark red haze.]]
[[Silent on purpose: the debris drifting on, with nothing said.]]
[[Hold: 4s.]]
Debris and embers drift past.

[[Pic: Wide: red sparks thin out across the foreground; far off at the right, one small intact orange-striped ship hangs alone.]]
[[Silent on purpose: the survivors, small and far away, before the count of the dead.]]
[[Hold: 4s.]]
One ship remains in the distance.

[[Sentence: flash-s013]]
[[Pic: Close among the wreckage: a torn arc of the Hesperos habitation ring, rows of round window frames dark and empty, red glints behind it; no bodies, numbers or text.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Two thousand people.

[[Sentence: flash-s014]]
[[Pic: Close: frost-covered cylindrical embryo-storage capsules with soft blue lights tumble among debris against a dark red background; no visible embryos, labels or graphic detail.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A third of all our embryos.

[[Sentence: flash-s015]]
[[Pic: Exterior: a surviving orange-striped ship drifts through a stream of torn hull plates lit orange along their edges, a red glow beyond; fragments pass close beside it.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, pieces of the Hesperos flew past us, only a few miles away.

INT. COMMAND DECK - NIGHT

[[Sentence: flash-s016]]
[[Pic: Interior wide from the command deck: a large round window with a railing before it; one survivor ship large at the left edge, the other only a tiny distant point at the right in star-flecked black space; dark consoles at the edges, nobody visible.]]

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So the command moved our two ships far apart, so that one hit could never take both.
